.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt APPLEMACAUDIO 4 evbarm
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm applemacaudio
.Nd Apple Silicon Mac speakers and their protection interlock
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "applemacaudio* at fdt? pass 5"
.Cd "applemca* at fdt?"
.Cd "appleadmac* at fdt? pass 4"
.Cd "applenco* at fdt? pass 2"
.Cd "tasamp* at iic?"
.Cd "cscodec* at iic?"
.Cd "audio* at audiobus?"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver attaches the speaker array of Apple Silicon Macs as an
.Xr audio 4
playback device, the amplifiers' voltage and current sense as a
second, capture-only
.Xr audio 4
device, and the headphone jack as a third device whose
.Dq outputs.headphones
mixer control sets the codec's volume; it also records a headset's
microphone when one is plugged in, see
.Xr cscodec 4 .
The headphone jack does not depend on the speaker interlock below.
.Pp
The speakers have no protection of their own and are destroyed within
seconds by a loud enough signal.
Protection is left to a userland daemon, such as speakersafetyd, that
models their temperature from the sense data and turns them down as they
heat.
The kernel's part is an interlock:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Each amplifier enforces an output level cap for the machine, and a lock
that holds its volume at mute; see
.Xr tasamp 4 .
The lock is closed at attach.
.It
Writing the magic value 0xdec1be15 to
.Va hw.applemacaudio0.speaker_unlock
opens the lock.
It closes again after 250 ms of playback without such a write; the time
counts only while playing, and playback that starts after a pause gets
only what was left.
A daemon that exits or hangs therefore leaves the speakers muted.
.It
Machines on which the speaker model has not been validated get no
speaker output.
.El
.Pp
User volume stays in the audio layer; the per-speaker volumes below are
for the daemon.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va hw.applemacaudio0.compatible
The machine, as the first device tree compatible string, for example
.Dq apple,j293 .
.It Va hw.applemacaudio0.sense_audio
The
.Xr audio 4
device recording the sense data, for example
.Dq audio1 .
It records 16-bit samples, two channels per speaker, current before
voltage, in the order of the device tree codec list, and only while the
speakers are playing.
.It Va hw.applemacaudio0.speaker_rate
The speaker sample rate, or 0 when idle.
.It Va hw.applemacaudio0.speaker_unlock
Write the magic value to open the lock; reads 1 while open.
.It Va hw.applemacaudio0.amp_gain_max
The amplifier output level cap, in hundredths of a dBV.
.It Va hw.applemacaudio0.speaker\&. Ns Ar name Ns Va \&.volume
The volume of one speaker, in hundredths of a dB, 0 or below.
.Ar name
is the device tree
.Dq sound-name-prefix
of its amplifier, lower case with other characters replaced by
underscores, for example
.Dq left_front .
.El
.Pp
Only the super-user can write these.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr audio 4 ,
.Xr cscodec 4 ,
.Xr ssmamp 4 ,
.Xr tasamp 4 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Nx 12.0 .
.Sh CAVEATS
Machines with the SN012776 amplifier, Apple's variant of the TI TAS2764,
do not play: its differences are not documented and
.Xr tasamp 4
keeps it in shutdown.
Those with the TAS5770L play once
.Xr speakersafetyd 8
unlocks them.
.Pp
The per-machine output caps, the timeout and the magic value are taken
from the Asahi Linux project, as is the time-division layout of the
speaker ports.
